@database "llm_gemini"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: llm_gemini.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Google Gemini LLM providers"
@{h1}Google Gemini LLM providers

This plugin lets agents talk to Google's Gemini models directly, without a
gateway in between: over Gemini's own REST API, or through Google's official
@{tt}google-genai@{utt} SDK. It also serves Gemini batch jobs and Gemini text
to speech. It is an LLM provider plugin: it has no tools, no hooks and no
panel. You use it by naming one of its providers in a model entry of
@{tt}config/llm.yaml@{utt}; agents reach that entry through a profile.

@{table}
Provider name | Kind | Endpoint
gemini | chat | POST <base_url>/models/<model>:streamGenerateContent (streamed) or :generateContent, over httpx
gemini_sdk | chat | the same API through the google-genai SDK
gemini | batch | the Gemini Batch API, through the SDK
gemini_tts | text to speech | generateContent with an audio answer, through the SDK
@{body}

How models, profiles and fallback chains work in general is in
@{" LLM models and profiles " link scarabhive/llm}; this manual covers what
these providers do with an entry.

@{code}
For users (the operator writing llm.yaml)
  @{"  Which provider to take  " link routes}
  @{"  Model entry keys  " link keys}
  @{"  Timeouts and streaming  " link timeouts}
  @{"  Retries  " link retries}
  @{"  Thinking  " link reasoning}
  @{"  Usage, cost and the hooks  " link usage}
  @{"  Errors  " link errors}
  @{"  Batch and speech  " link batch}

For agents and developers
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node routes "Which provider to take"
@{h1}Which provider to take

Both chat providers send the same request -- the same message conversion,
generation settings, safety settings and thinking configuration -- and
return the same answer shape. They differ in the transport and in what you
can see and set:

@{table}
Difference | gemini | gemini_sdk
transport | httpx, one connection per request | the google-genai SDK (aiohttp when installed, else httpx)
base_url | used | ignored: always Google's endpoint
timeouts | connect, read, write, pool | only read (see @{" Timeouts and streaming " link timeouts})
TLS verification | follows the app's setting | the SDK's default
request in the message debugger | the full request body | only counts: contents_count, has_tools, has_system
tool call without a thought signature in the history | sent without one | sent with Google's bypass token
MALFORMED_FUNCTION_CALL | retried when streamed or not | retried only when streamed
rate-limit retries | up to 3, sharing the attempts | up to 2, with 0-50 % jitter
@{body}

The shipped @{tt}gemini-3-*@{utt} entries use gemini_sdk.

@{h2}Direct or via OpenRouter

Gemini is also reachable through an OpenRouter entry of
@{" OpenAI-compatible providers " link llm_openai_compat/routes}. One
difference decides which to take: @{b}safety_settings only work on the
direct route.@{ub} OpenRouter drops the field on @{tt}/responses@{utt}
(measured 2026-09-01), and its own default sets every harm category to off.
An entry that needs its own thresholds uses this plugin.
@endnode

@node keys "Model entry keys"
@{h1}Model entry keys

The keys of a model entry these providers read. Everything else in
@{tt}LLMModelConfig@{utt} is either handled by the core (@{tt}extends@{utt},
profiles) or not read here. The keys of other providers' dialects
(@{tt}tool_schema_dialect@{utt}, @{tt}assistant_reasoning_field@{utt},
@{tt}reasoning_details_mode@{utt}, @{tt}thinking_request_shape@{utt},
@{tt}stream_silence_timeout@{utt}, @{tt}provider_affinity_minutes@{utt})
are logged as "not wired ... will be ignored" when the client is built.

@{table}
Key | Default | What the clients do with it
provider | -- | gemini or gemini_sdk
model | -- | the model id, e.g. gemini-3.1-pro-preview
api_key | GEMINI_API_KEY, else GOOGLE_API_KEY | building the client fails without one
base_url | https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta | gemini only; gemini_sdk ignores it
context_window | the model-config default 32768, 200000 if 0 | only reported to the core, never sent
max_tokens | none | generationConfig.maxOutputTokens; null sends nothing
temperature | none | generationConfig.temperature, only when set; Gemini 3.6 Flash and later ignore it, upcoming models reject it
thinking_level | none | thinkingConfig.thinkingLevel (Gemini 3); see @{" Thinking " link reasoning}
thinking_budget | none | thinkingConfig.thinkingBudget (Gemini 2.5); 0 turns thinking off
include_thoughts | none | thinkingConfig.includeThoughts: whether thought summaries come back
safety_settings | none | a map category: threshold, sent as safetySettings whenever set
capabilities | all defaults | streaming decides the path; structured_output allows response formats
request_timeout, httpx_timeouts | see @{" Timeouts and streaming " link timeouts} |
parallel_tool_calls | -- | accepted, never sent: Gemini has no such field
prompt_cache_key, prompt_cache_mode, ... | -- | not read: Gemini caches implicitly (see @{" Usage, cost and the hooks " link usage})
@{body}

@{code yaml}
llm_system:
  models:
    gemini-3-pro:
      provider: gemini_sdk
      model: gemini-3.1-pro-preview
      api_key: ${GOOGLE_API_KEY}
      request_timeout: 240
      max_tokens: 16384
      thinking_level: high
      include_thoughts: true
      safety_settings:
        HARM_CATEGORY_DANGEROUS_CONTENT: BLOCK_ONLY_HIGH
        HARM_CATEGORY_HARASSMENT: BLOCK_ONLY_HIGH
    gemini-3-pro-nostream:
      extends: gemini-3-pro
      include_thoughts: false
      capabilities:
        streaming: false
@{body}

Note that @{tt}${VAR}@{utt} of an unset variable expands to an empty
string, which counts as "no key" -- as does a key of only spaces: the
environment variables above apply then. A key is sent without surrounding
spaces.
@endnode

@node timeouts "Timeouts and streaming"
@{h1}Timeouts and streaming

@{h2}Streaming or not

A model streams unless its entry says @{tt}capabilities.streaming:
false@{utt}. Streaming lets the web UI show the answer as it arrives, and it
changes what the read timeout means. With it off, the client waits for the
whole answer in one response.

@{h2}The read timeout

Both providers take their read timeout from the same place:

@{bullet}a @{tt}read@{utt} in the entry's @{tt}httpx_timeouts@{utt} wins;
@{bullet}else the entry's own @{tt}request_timeout@{utt};
@{bullet}else 180 s.

An entry without its own @{tt}httpx_timeouts@{utt} gets the system default
@{tt}llm_system.httpx_timeouts@{utt} from the core, whose read is the entry's
@{tt}request_timeout@{utt} when the entry sets one (the shipped
@{tt}config/llm.yaml@{utt} sets read 180 s). A @{tt}request_timeout@{utt}
that only comes from the model-config default (120) does not count.

@{b}Streamed:@{ub} the longest gap between two pieces of the stream -- a
longer gap is a stalled stream and is retried. @{b}Not streamed:@{ub} the
longest wait for the whole answer, so a long answer needs a long read
timeout.

@{h2}gemini

The other three come from @{tt}httpx_timeouts@{utt} too; a key it leaves
out falls back to connect 10 s, write 10 s, pool 5 s.

@{h2}gemini_sdk

The SDK is built without a timeout of its own. The client enforces the read
timeout itself: for the first response of a stream and between its pieces,
and for the whole answer when not streamed. Connect, write and pool are not
used. When the connection cannot be opened, the SDK itself waits 1-10 s and
tries once more before the error reaches the client.

@{h2}How a stream ends

@{bullet}A stream that ends is the answer, whatever finish reason it had --
except MALFORMED_FUNCTION_CALL and MAX_TOKENS, which are retried (see
@{" Retries " link retries}).
@{bullet}A stream that stalls, or repeats the same thought (five identical
thought pieces of 20 characters or more among the last 20), or sends 100
pieces of thinking in a row with nothing else, is given up and retried.
@{bullet}A run cancelled while the stream is out stops when the next piece
arrives (gemini) or within half a second (gemini_sdk). The cancellation
reaches the agent as a cancellation, never as an answer.
@{bullet}A gemini request that is not streamed notices a cancelled run only
between attempts, or when its task is cancelled; gemini_sdk checks every
half second.
@endnode

@node retries "Retries"
@{h1}Retries

Every retry is reported to the post_llm_response hooks as its own row
(@{tt}[RETRY n/m] ...@{utt}, finish reason @{tt}retry@{utt}) and most as a
status line in the chat. A cancelled run interrupts every wait. A retry
starts from scratch: when the caller has already received pieces of the
failed attempt, the stream first sends one @{tt}{"type":
"stream_restart"}@{utt} chunk, and the caller drops what it has shown of
that attempt.

Every retry also asks for less thinking: a thinking_level above minimal
becomes low, and a thinking_budget is halved per attempt (below 512 it
becomes 0, thinking off). After a MALFORMED_FUNCTION_CALL the retries force
function calling (@{tt}mode ANY@{utt}); a 400 on such a retry drops that
again.

@{h2}gemini

Up to four attempts in all, shared by every kind of retry below.

@{table}
What happened | What the client does
HTTP 5xx | retry after 1, 2, 4 s; then LLMServerError
HTTP 400 "too many states" | retry after 2, 4, 8 s
rate limit (the error names 429, rate limit, quota, resource_exhausted or too many requests) | retry after the delay Google names plus 2 s, else 60 s; then LLMQuotaExhaustedError if it says quota or exhausted, else LLMRateLimitError
any other HTTP 4xx (not 408) | no retry: httpx.HTTPStatusError at once, so the fallback and the block of a dead key or model come without delay
timeout, stall, dropped connection, thinking loop | retry after 1, 2, 4 s; then LLMConnectionError
MALFORMED_FUNCTION_CALL (streamed: only without text and tool calls) | retry after 1, 2, 3 s with mode ANY; the last one is handed over as it is
MAX_TOKENS without a tool call, streamed | retry after 1 s; the last one is handed over as it is
@{body}

@{h2}gemini_sdk

Up to four attempts in all.

@{table}
What happened | What the client does
rate limit (as above) | up to 2 retries after the delay Google names plus 2 s, else 60 s, plus 0-50 % jitter; then LLMQuotaExhaustedError / LLMRateLimitError
"too many states" | retry after 2, 4, 8 s
500 INTERNAL | retry after 5, 10, 20 s; then LLMServerError
other 5xx | retry after 1, 2, 4 s; then LLMServerError
any other HTTP 4xx (not 408) | no retry: httpx.HTTPStatusError at once
timeout, stall, connection error, thinking loop | retry after 1, 2, 4 s; then LLMConnectionError
MALFORMED_FUNCTION_CALL without a tool call, or with a tool call whose arguments are empty (streamed) | retry after 1, 2, 3 s with mode ANY
MAX_TOKENS without a tool call, streamed | retry after 1 s
@{body}

The agent server's own fallback chain (@{tt}llm_profile@{utt} with more than
one profile) takes over where these retries end; see
@{" Writing an agent " link scarabhive/agentdev}.
@endnode

@node reasoning "Thinking"
@{h1}Thinking

@{h2}Asking for it

Gemini 3 models always think; @{tt}thinking_level@{utt} says how much
(minimal, low, medium, high). The config also accepts OpenRouter's levels:
none becomes minimal, xhigh and max become high. Gemini 2.5 models take
@{tt}thinking_budget@{utt} in tokens instead; 0 turns thinking off, a
negative value is not sent. Without either, the model's default applies.
@{tt}include_thoughts: true@{utt} asks for the model's thought summaries
back; without it Gemini thinks just the same but shows nothing.

@{h2}Watching it

Thought summaries stream to the chat as they arrive, as thinking (the
agent server's thinking deltas): the chat shows them apart from the answer,
and the agent server's reasoning-loop watch and llm_progress hooks see them.
The stored message keeps them apart too: the answer as its content,
the thinking as @{tt}reasoning_content@{utt}. Thinking is not sent back on
later turns; the thought signatures on tool calls are (see
@{" What the model sees " link model}).

@{h2}Usage

Gemini counts the thinking apart from the answer and bills it as output.
@{tt}completion_tokens@{utt} is both, and the thinking is also given as
@{tt}completion_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens@{utt} -- a detail of the
completion, never computed from it.
@endnode

@node usage "Usage, cost and the hooks"
@{h1}Usage, cost and the hooks

Each request is reported to the @{tt}pre_llm_request@{utt} hooks before it
is sent, and to the @{tt}post_llm_response@{utt} hooks when it ends. The
message debugger, the OpenTelemetry chat span and the cost figures are built
from these reports (@{" Hooks " link scarabhive/hooks},
@{" The capture hooks " link message_debugger/hooks},
@{" What gets exported " link otel/spans},
@{" What it records " link context_usage_tracker/hooks}). gemini sends the
request body with the report (the URL without the key); gemini_sdk only
counts: contents, whether tools and a system instruction were sent.

@{h2}One report per ending

@{table}
The request ended with | post_llm_response carries
an answer | usage; finish reason (gemini not streamed: Gemini's own, e.g. STOP, with response_data; gemini streamed: Gemini's last one; gemini_sdk: tool_calls or stop)
an answer without content (a blocked prompt) | usage, and on gemini the response_data with its promptFeedback
a retry | an error @{tt}[RETRY n/m] ...@{utt}, finish reason retry, no usage
a refusal after the retries | the raised error's text (@{tt}HTTP 404: ...@{utt}, @{tt}Rate limit exceeded: ...@{utt}, @{tt}ReadTimeout: ...@{utt}, ...)
a cancellation while the stream was out | the error @{tt}Request cancelled during streaming@{utt}, or @{tt}cancelled@{utt} when the task was cancelled
a cancellation of a request not streamed | @{tt}cancelled@{utt}; on gemini_sdk a cancelled run gives @{tt}Request cancelled during execution@{utt}, also for a stream whose first piece had not come
a cancellation during a retry wait | the error @{tt}Request cancelled during retry wait@{utt}, after the retry's row
a cancellation before it was sent | the error @{tt}Request cancelled before attempt@{utt}
a stream the caller stopped reading | the error @{tt}stream abandoned by the caller@{utt}
anything unexpected | the exception's text
@{body}

A streamed ending that is not an answer carries the usage that had arrived
by then. A cancelled stream is billed for what Gemini generated, but its
usage usually comes last, so the report mostly has none.

@{h2}The usage

@{bullet}From Gemini's usageMetadata: @{tt}prompt_tokens@{utt} (cached
tokens included), @{tt}completion_tokens@{utt} (answer plus thinking),
@{tt}total_tokens@{utt}; @{tt}prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens@{utt}
and @{tt}completion_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens@{utt} as the subsets
when there are any. A streamed answer takes the last chunk that carried
usage, with or without content.
@{bullet}Caching is Gemini's implicit caching: nothing is marked in the
request, and the cached tokens are what Gemini reports. Cache markers the
core put into the prompt are removed before sending.
@{bullet}Cost: the core prices the tokens from
@{tt}config/llm_pricing.yaml@{utt}.
@endnode

@node errors "Errors"
@{h1}Errors

What reaches the agent server when a request cannot be answered. The server
decides from the type whether to fall back to the next profile of the chain
(@{" Writing an agent " link scarabhive/agentdev}); a plain Exception ends
the run.

@{table}
Raised | When
LLMRateLimitError | a rate limit after the retries
LLMQuotaExhaustedError | the same, with "quota" or "exhausted" in the error
LLMServerError | 5xx after the retries
httpx.HTTPStatusError | any other 4xx, without retries (except the two 400s retried above); its status is Google's (gemini_sdk too)
LLMConnectionError | timeout, stall, dropped connection or thinking loop after the retries
Exception | anything else after the retries, e.g. an answer that is no JSON (@{tt}Gemini request failed: ...@{utt}, @{tt}Gemini SDK request failed: ...@{utt})
asyncio.CancelledError | the run was cancelled
ValueError at build time | no API key
ImportError at build time | gemini_sdk without the google-genai package
@{body}

An answer cut off by its output cap is handed over with what it has, after
the MAX_TOKENS retries when streamed. An answer the safety settings blocked
comes back empty.
@endnode

@node batch "Batch and speech"
@{h1}Batch and speech

@{h2}Batch

A model entry with @{tt}provider: batch@{utt} and @{tt}batch_provider:
gemini@{utt} runs its requests through the Gemini Batch API: the requests go
inline in one job, the job is polled, the answers come back inline. Each
request carries its messages, system instruction, tools, max_tokens,
thinking settings and safety settings -- no temperature. Network errors
while submitting, polling or fetching are retried three times (1 s
doubling, at most 30 s). A batch answer has the same shape as a chat answer:
thinking as @{tt}reasoning_content@{utt}, thinking tokens as output. The key
is the entry's @{tt}api_key@{utt}, GEMINI_API_KEY or GOOGLE_API_KEY; without
one there is no batch backend and the requests run one by one. The queue and
its settings belong to the core; see
@{" Batch Monitor " link batch_monitor/main}.

@{h2}Speech

@{tt}provider: gemini_tts@{utt} in @{tt}tts_models@{utt} synthesises with a
Gemini TTS model.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
model | gemini-2.5-flash-preview-tts | the speech model
api_key | GEMINI_API_KEY, else GOOGLE_API_KEY | building the client fails without one
voice | Kore | the voice when the caller names none; one of Gemini's 30 prebuilt voices
request_timeout | 300 | seconds per attempt
max_retries | 3 | retries on 5xx, 429 and transport errors, after 2, 4, 8 ... s (at most 30 s)
@{body}

@{bullet}Audio comes back as raw PCM, 24 kHz, 16 bit mono.
@{bullet}Several speakers in one call are supported, each with its own
voice.
@{bullet}A style prompt goes in front of the text: the TTS models refuse a
system instruction.
@{bullet}The usage is reported like a chat call's.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}Messages

@{bullet}All system messages become one systemInstruction, after a fixed
first paragraph: "CRITICAL: When calling functions, output the function name
exactly as defined. Do NOT prepend 'default_api.' or any other namespace.
Always generate valid JSON for function arguments. Properly escape all
special characters in JSON strings (quotes, backslashes, newlines)."
@{bullet}A developer note goes as a user turn wrapped as a note: the API
has no other role for it, and a model entry asking for more is told so in
the log.
@{bullet}Assistant turns become the model's; every other role a user turn.
Consecutive user turns are merged, and the answers to parallel tool calls
go back in one turn, as Gemini requires.
@{bullet}Tool results go as function responses: the result's JSON, or
@{tt}{"result": <text>}@{utt}. Images a tool returns travel in the same turn.
@{bullet}Tool calls and results of tools the agent no longer has become text
summaries, so Gemini does not refuse the history.
@{bullet}Text, images and audio (as data URLs) and attached text files are
sent; an external URL is dropped with a warning, other parts are not sent.
@{bullet}A request over 100 MB has its oldest inline data replaced by
placeholders until it is under 80 MB.
@{bullet}A request with nothing left to send gets the user turn "Continue
with the task."

@{h2}Thought signatures

Gemini 3 marks each tool call with a thought signature that must come back
on the next turn. It is stored on the tool call
(@{tt}extra_content.google.thought_signature@{utt}); parallel calls without
one get the first call's. gemini sends back the signatures it has;
gemini_sdk sends Google's bypass token for a call without one, e.g. after a
switch from another model.

@{h2}Tools

Tool schemas go out as function declarations, with the keywords Gemini
refuses removed. Function calling is automatic, and forced (mode ANY) only
on the retries after a MALFORMED_FUNCTION_CALL.

@{h2}Structured output

A response format is sent as @{tt}responseMimeType@{utt} (JSON mode) plus
@{tt}responseJsonSchema@{utt} for a schema, when the entry declares
@{tt}capabilities.structured_output@{utt}. Gemini 3 takes a schema beside
function declarations, earlier models refuse the combination. A client
asked for a format the entry does not declare refuses the call rather than
return free text.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

Nothing to enable: the core finds LLM provider plugins by their
@{tt}plugin.toml@{utt} (@{tt}type = ["llm-provider"]@{utt}) and loads
@{tt}provider.py@{utt} when a model entry names one of its providers. There
is no entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt} and no allowlist line. A
misspelt provider name fails when the configuration loads.

@{table}
Manifest key | Names
provides | gemini, gemini_sdk
provides_batch | gemini
provides_tts | gemini_tts
@{body}

@{h2}Dependencies

@{tt}google-genai@{utt} (1.50 or later) for gemini_sdk, the batch backend
and speech; gemini_sdk imports it on its first call, not when the agent is
built.
gemini needs only @{tt}httpx@{utt}, which the core requires anyway.

@{h2}Where things live

Nothing on disk. Changes to @{tt}llm.yaml@{utt} reach an agent when it
builds its client, i.e. after a restart.
@endnode
